%% Domain-Driven Design (DDD) gate
%%
%% Core DDD principles enforced here:
%%   - Ubiquitous Language: names must come from the domain model, not the implementation layer
%%   - Bounded Context: each module owns its domain concepts; no leaking across context boundaries
%%   - Strategic Design: avoid anemic models, god objects, and generic catch-all names
%%
%% Bad examples: UserManager, DataProcessor, InfoHelper, utils.rs, do_thing(), handle()
%% Good examples: ChecklistGate, SessionState, HookEvent, cel_eval.rs, advance_state(), block_push()

stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> domain_names
    domain_names --> no_generic_names
    no_generic_names --> bounded_context
    bounded_context --> no_utils_folders
    no_utils_folders --> [*]

    domain_names    : Do all new files and modules use domain language (not technical jargon like `manager`, `handler`, `utils`)?
    no_generic_names: Are all new functions and types named after what they mean in the domain (no `data`, `info`, `stuff`, `process`, `do_thing`)?
    bounded_context : Does each changed module stay within its bounded context — no cross-domain leakage or god objects?
    no_utils_folders: Are new files placed in a domain module (e.g. `checklist/`, `session/`) and NOT in a catch-all folder like `utils/`, `helpers/`, `common/`, or `shared/`?
